# Line endings are a property of this repository, not of whoever cloned it.
#
# Git for Windows sets core.autocrlf=true in its system config; most other
# installs leave it unset. Without this file, what gets stored in a blob depends
# on which of those checked it in, and `git status` can report every line of an
# untouched file as changed. Pinning it here takes the question away from
# individual developer configuration.
#
# eol=lf rather than plain text=auto, because LF is wanted in the working tree
# as well: CI runs shell scripts on Linux and macOS, and on Windows every tool
# this project needs -- MSVC, CMake, Git Bash, any usable editor -- reads LF
# without complaint.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Files that must be delivered byte for byte, whatever their extension suggests.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.pdf binary
*.bin binary
*.der binary
*.p12 binary
*.a   binary
*.lib binary
*.o   binary
*.obj binary
*.exe binary

# Fuzz corpus inputs are raw bytes that happen to have no extension. text=auto
# guesses, and an input it guesses wrong about gets its line endings rewritten
# -- which silently changes the bytes the fuzzer replays, and with them what
# the corpus covers.
# One directory per target, so this matches the inputs without matching the
# documentation that sits beside them -- `binary` is a macro for -diff -merge
# -text, and a README carrying it would lose its diff.
tests/fuzz/corpus/*/** binary
